<p>A ₹5 lakh personal loan feels manageable until you see the total cost. At 14% annual rate for 3 years, your EMI is approximately ₹17,090 and total interest paid is ₹1.15 lakh — you repay ₹6.15 lakh for a ₹5 lakh loan. At 18% (a rate many applicants with CIBIL scores under 700 actually receive), that same ₹5 lakh costs ₹1.51 lakh in interest — a ₹36,000 penalty for a weaker credit profile. At 24% (the upper end for unsecured personal loans), total interest climbs to ₹2.06 lakh on that same ₹5 lakh. The EMI advertised by lenders hides this total cost, which this calculator makes visible before you sign.</p>

<p>One critical fact most borrowers miss: <strong>personal loan interest is not tax-deductible</strong> under any provision of the Income Tax Act for salaried individuals borrowing for personal use. Unlike a home loan where Section 24(b) allows up to ₹2 lakh in annual interest deduction (old tax regime, self-occupied property only), personal loan interest provides zero tax benefit — the full interest burden comes from post-tax income. If you are considering a personal loan partly to save tax, that logic does not apply here. Use the <a href="/india/loan-eligibility-calculator/">Loan Eligibility Calculator</a> to check whether this EMI fits within your income before applying, and the <a href="/india/loan-prepayment-calculator/">Loan Prepayment Calculator</a> to model interest saved by early repayment.</p>
